<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann and Wendelin of Speyer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1468">1468</a>–<a href="/years/1477">1477</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Giovanni and Vindelino da Spira, de Speier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann and Wendelin of Speyer were German brothers who worked as printers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1468 to 1477, among the first to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Venetian Senate granted Johann a five-year monopoly of printing, and he began in 1469 with a hundred copies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s letters, followed by the first printed edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>'s Natural History and an edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>. Johann died of the plague in 1470 while printing Augustine's City of God, and Wendelin finished it and issued over seventy major works until 1477. Johann is credited with the first numbering of leaves in Arabic numerals, and the brothers are sometimes named as originators of roman type.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-and-wendelin-of-speyer.jpg" alt="Johann and Wendelin of Speyer" width="330" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pliny the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_page_from_the_Editio_Princeps_of_the_Pliny's_&quot;Historia_Naturalis&quot;.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
